{"t":9232,"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"af603905276e660fe","run_id":"0mwm27n68-5a47f553","tool_use_id":"toolu_013jcjSV6ijsa9xT9ERomjte","status":"completed","output_file":"C:\\Users\\spesce\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\a3af3560164d967d\\00fd7e89-3d8b-462b-a750-92ea96c2d04a\\tasks\\af603905276e660fe.output","summary":"The compass began in China, where people used lodestone, a naturally magnetized iron ore, for divination as early as the Han dynasty. Early devices were spoon-shaped pieces of lodestone set on a smooth bronze plate, and they were used to align buildings and plan cities according to geomancy. Around the eleventh century, Chinese scholars described magnetized iron needles that floated in water or hung from silk thread. Shen Kuo, writing in the Dream Pool Essays, noted that such needles pointed slightly off true north. This is one of the earliest observations of magnetic declination.\n\nBy the late twelfth century, mariners in the Mediterranean and Europe were using the compass for navigation. Alexander Neckam wrote about it around 1190. Sailors would magnetize a needle by rubbing it on lodestone and float it in a bowl of water. This let them keep their bearings under cloudy skies and at night, when stars could not guide them.\n\nOver the following centuries, Europeans made the instrument steadier and more precise. The needle was mounted on a pivot, and a card marked with the compass points was attached to it. Later, the gimbal kept the compass level on a pitching ship. The compass helped make the long ocean voyages of the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries possible. In the twentieth century, gyrocompasses and electronic navigation systems supplemented the magnetic compass, but it remains a trusted backup today.\n\nWORKER_TOKEN=61840273","usage":{"total_tokens":6063,"tool_uses":1,"duration_ms":7183},"uuid":"15d40038-eb5e-495f-8e13-9975fd9fb8da","session_id":"00fd7e89-3d8b-462b-a750-92ea96c2d04a"}
