{"t":9427,"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a0cea6574292ab75a","run_id":"0mwm27n88-1e5b97db","tool_use_id":"toolu_012TZgYwMkeq5JcgUfJun5pW","status":"completed","output_file":"C:\\Users\\spesce\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\a3af3560164d967d\\00fd7e89-3d8b-462b-a750-92ea96c2d04a\\tasks\\a0cea6574292ab75a.output","summary":"The sextant is a navigation instrument for measuring the angle between a celestial body, usually the sun or a star, and the horizon. Its roots go back to earlier tools such as the astrolabe and the backstaff. In the early 1730s, two inventors working independently arrived at the key idea. John Hadley in England and Thomas Godfrey in Philadelphia each devised a reflecting instrument built around a double-mirror system. That design let the observer see the horizon and the celestial object in the same view. Because the two images stayed together, the measurement stayed accurate even on a pitching ship.\n\nHadley's octant spanned one eighth of a circle but could measure up to ninety degrees, thanks to the doubling effect of reflection. Later, sailors wanted to measure larger angles, such as the lunar distances used to find longitude. Around 1757, Captain John Campbell suggested extending the arc to a sixth of a circle, and the sextant was born. Jesse Ramsden's precision dividing engine, developed in the 1770s, made finely graduated scales possible and improved reliability.\n\nThe sextant became essential once navigators combined it with accurate chronometers and nautical almanacs. For about two centuries it was the main tool for finding position at sea. Even after radio navigation and GPS arrived, many mariners and naval academies kept teaching its use as a dependable backup that needs no electricity.\n\nWORKER_TOKEN=29537106","usage":{"total_tokens":6047,"tool_uses":1,"duration_ms":7060},"uuid":"36683c8b-0572-40ef-9712-56d095b504b4","session_id":"00fd7e89-3d8b-462b-a750-92ea96c2d04a"}
